WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.566
Suppose you have a sorted list of 1,000,000 numbers,

00:00:03.566 --> 00:00:07.020
and you want to know whether a certain number is in it.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.610
One way is to look at the numbers one by one.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.210
If the number isn't there, that's 1,000,000 looks.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.768
Another way is binary search, from Lecture 6.

00:00:17.768 --> 00:00:20.120
It halves the list at every step.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.040
For the same 1,000,000 numbers, it needs just 20 looks.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:29.504
Now buy a computer 1,000 times faster, and keep the first method.

00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:32.651
It still looks at all 1,000,000 numbers.

00:00:32.651 --> 00:00:37.135
A faster computer makes a slow algorithm a bit less slow.

00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:40.990
A better algorithm can turn a year into a second.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.370
So how do we tell a good algorithm from a bad one, before we've even run it?

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.620
That's what this lecture is about.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.123
Welcome to Lecture 9 of Introduction to AI Programming:

00:00:53.123 --> 00:00:55.560
running time, searching and sorting.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.009
In this first part, we'll see why a stopwatch isn't enough,

00:01:00.009 --> 00:01:05.150
how to count operations instead, and how fast seven common functions grow.

00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:07.263
First, two terms.

00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:12.736
A data structure is a systematic way of organising and accessing data.

00:01:12.736 --> 00:01:16.333
A Python list is one; a dictionary is another.

00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:22.713
An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure for performing a task in a finite amount of time.

00:01:22.713 --> 00:01:24.723
Binary search is an algorithm.

00:01:25.073 --> 00:01:29.043
Every branch of computer science uses them, and so does AI:

00:01:29.043 --> 00:01:35.233
training a model is an algorithm running over data structures that hold millions of numbers.

00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:39.285
What makes an algorithm good? Mainly two things:

00:01:39.285 --> 00:01:45.685
its running time, how long it takes, and its space usage, how much memory it needs.

00:01:45.685 --> 00:01:48.383
This lecture is about running time.

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:54.327
The direct way to find out how fast a program is: run it, and time it.

00:01:54.677 --> 00:01:57.708
This program from the slides reads the clock,

00:01:57.708 --> 00:02:01.480
runs a loop that prints every tenth number up to 20,000,

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.231
and reads the clock again.

00:02:03.231 --> 00:02:06.397
The difference is the time elapsed, in seconds.

00:02:06.747 --> 00:02:09.693
Run it twice, and you get two different numbers.

00:02:09.693 --> 00:02:12.700
Run it on a friend's laptop, and you get a third.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.427
Most of the time goes into print, which is slow compared with the arithmetic.

00:02:17.777 --> 00:02:20.653
To see how running time depends on the input,

00:02:20.653 --> 00:02:23.401
we run the program on inputs of many sizes,

00:02:23.401 --> 00:02:27.555
and plot each run as a point: input size across, running time up.

00:02:27.555 --> 00:02:30.942
The points rise with the size, roughly along a curve,

00:02:30.942 --> 00:02:34.777
with some scatter from whatever else the computer was doing.

00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:39.607
Experiments are useful, but they have three problems.

00:02:39.957 --> 00:02:42.285
First, they depend on the machine.

00:02:42.285 --> 00:02:49.477
Two algorithms can only be compared fairly if they were timed on the same hardware and the same software.

00:02:49.827 --> 00:02:52.910
Second, they only cover the inputs you tried.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:58.667
An input you didn't test might be much slower, and it might be the one that matters.

00:02:59.017 --> 00:03:02.191
Third, you need the whole program first.

00:03:02.191 --> 00:03:04.017
You can't time an idea.

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:06.484
Now ask a different question.

00:03:06.484 --> 00:03:11.011
In that timing program, how many times does the loop body run,

00:03:11.011 --> 00:03:13.567
and how many times is print called?

00:03:14.117 --> 00:03:18.731
Range of 1 to 20,000 gives 19,999 numbers,

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:23.346
and one in 10 of them is a multiple of 10:

00:03:23.346 --> 00:03:24.774
1,999 prints.

00:03:24.774 --> 00:03:31.037
Those counts are the same on every computer in the world.

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:34.439
That's the idea behind algorithm analysis.

00:03:34.439 --> 00:03:39.154
Instead of timing a program, we count its steps, following three principles.

00:03:39.504 --> 00:03:42.729
Principle 1: count primitive operations.

00:03:42.729 --> 00:03:49.504
These are simple steps that each take a small, fixed amount of time on any computer.

00:03:50.104 --> 00:03:52.370
Assigning a name to an object.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.315
Looking up the object a name refers to.

00:03:55.315 --> 00:03:57.128
An arithmetic operation.

00:03:57.128 --> 00:03:58.790
Comparing two numbers.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.736
Reading one element of a list by index.

00:04:01.736 --> 00:04:04.984
Calling a function, and returning from one.

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:09.277
If an algorithm performs a certain number of these operations,

00:04:09.277 --> 00:04:14.682
its running time is roughly that number times a constant that depends on the machine.

00:04:14.682 --> 00:04:19.134
Counting removes the machine, and keeps what belongs to the algorithm.

00:04:19.484 --> 00:04:23.714
Principle 2: count as a function of the input size.

00:04:23.714 --> 00:04:28.193
Bigger inputs take longer, so one number isn't enough.

00:04:28.193 --> 00:04:33.336
We write f(n): the number of operations on an input of size n,

00:04:33.336 --> 00:04:39.724
where n is the length of the list, or whatever measures how big the input is.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.420
Principle 3: focus on the worst case.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.696
Inputs of the same size can take different amounts of time.

00:04:48.696 --> 00:04:54.180
Sequential search looks at the elements of a list one by one, until it finds the target.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:59.011
If the target is the first element, it makes one comparison.

00:04:59.011 --> 00:05:00.579
That's the best case.

00:05:00.579 --> 00:05:06.927
If the target is last, or not there at all, it looks at every element: n comparisons.

00:05:06.927 --> 00:05:08.570
That's the worst case.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.704
The average case sounds the most useful.

00:05:11.704 --> 00:05:15.603
But average over which inputs, with which probabilities?

00:05:15.603 --> 00:05:17.343
So we use the worst case.

00:05:17.343 --> 00:05:23.400
It's easier to work out, and it's a guarantee: the algorithm is never slower than that.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:24.916
And one warning.

00:05:24.916 --> 00:05:27.906
Fewer lines of code does not mean faster.

00:05:27.906 --> 00:05:33.740
We count how many times each operation runs, not how many lines the program has.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:38.990
A one-line loop that runs 1,000,000 times does 1,000,000 times the work.

00:05:39.767 --> 00:05:44.127
Almost every running time in this course is one of seven functions.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:47.516
Constant, logarithm, and linear.

00:05:47.516 --> 00:05:54.639
Then n log n, quadratic (which is n squared), and cubic (which is n cubed).

00:05:54.639 --> 00:06:00.717
And finally exponential: 2 to the n, which grows fastest of all.

00:06:01.317 --> 00:06:04.738
In computer science, log n means log base 2:

00:06:04.738 --> 00:06:09.637
the number of times you can cut n in half before you reach one.

00:06:09.987 --> 00:06:14.501
This short program prints how fast each one grows,

00:06:14.501 --> 00:06:16.307
for n from 8 to 512.

00:06:16.657 --> 00:06:18.903
Read it column by column.

00:06:18.903 --> 00:06:23.127
Each time n doubles, log n goes up by just one.

00:06:23.127 --> 00:06:26.273
N log n a little more than doubles.

00:06:26.273 --> 00:06:30.497
N squared is multiplied by 4, and n cubed by 8.

00:06:30.847 --> 00:06:33.934
And 2 to the n is squared.

00:06:33.934 --> 00:06:37.258
2 to the 512 has 155 digits.

00:06:37.258 --> 00:06:42.127
No computer will ever do that many steps.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:48.126
Here are three algorithms that solve the same problem.

00:06:48.126 --> 00:06:55.287
On an input of size n, algorithm A performs 10 n log n plus 50 operations.

00:06:55.287 --> 00:06:58.577
B performs 2 n squared plus 100 n.

00:06:58.577 --> 00:07:00.900
And C performs 10,000 n.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:03.665
Pause the video and predict.

00:07:03.665 --> 00:07:06.252
Which is fastest when n is 10?

00:07:06.252 --> 00:07:09.530
And which is fastest when n is 10,000?

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.927
Here's the program, and its output.

00:07:12.927 --> 00:07:15.529
For n equal to 10, A is fastest,

00:07:15.529 --> 00:07:19.840
and C, with its huge constant, is by far the slowest.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:25.123
But for n equal to 10,000, C has overtaken B.

00:07:25.123 --> 00:07:30.384
And for 1,000,000, B is 200 times slower than C.

00:07:30.384 --> 00:07:34.550
B grows like n squared; C only like n.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:38.190
This loop finds the crossover.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:44.660
From n equal to 4,951 on, B does more work than C, forever.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:50.879
How fast a function grows decides which algorithm wins on large inputs.

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:54.930
The constants only decide where the crossover is.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:57.060
Let's sum it up.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:02.130
Timing a program depends on the machine, and on the inputs you tried.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:09.200
So we count primitive operations, as a function of the input size, in the worst case.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:14.190
And for large inputs, the growth rate decides, not the constants.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:15.999
Here's one to try.

00:08:15.999 --> 00:08:19.322
This function has a loop over range of n,

00:08:19.322 --> 00:08:22.807
and inside it another loop over range of n.

00:08:22.807 --> 00:08:26.940
How many times does the line inside both loops run?

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:32.083
In part 2, we'll make growth rates precise with big-O notation,

00:08:32.083 --> 00:08:35.290
and count every operation of a real program.

